commit 3a5ab43e23f2a6d98f7a9353c9975dfa548aebef
Author: Dmitry Sidorov <dmitry.sidorov@intel.com>
Date:   Fri Jul 10 12:38:47 2020 +0300

    Add support for SPV_INTEL_fpga_buffer_location extension
    
    This extension adds a function parameter decoration that is useful
    for FPGA targets. This decoration indicates that a particular global
    memory pointer can only access a particular physical memory location.
    Knowing this information at compile time can allow FPGA compilers to
    generate load store units of lower area for accesses done through such
    a pointer.
    
    Specification: https://github.com/intel/llvm/pull/2084
    
    Signed-off-by: Dmitry Sidorov <dmitry.sidorov@intel.com>

commit 945913cea0d5eb54225681540ac82c6924ce0ece
Author: Artem Gindinson <artem.gindinson@intel.com>
Date:   Wed Jul 22 16:29:31 2020 +0300

    [SYCL][FPGA] Translate the LSU controls built-in
    
    The LSU controls (interchangeable with "memory access controls")
    apply to a pointer variable; in LLVM IR, this is expressed via
    `llvm.ptr.annotation` intrinsics:
    `T* llvm.ptr.annotation.*(T* %, "{params:}{cache-size:..}")`.
    
    As far as the annotation algorithm is concerned, LSU controls go
    down the similar path as FPGA memory attributes. The primary
    differences are:
    1) the "on/off" parameters are encoded in a bitmask. Meanwhile,
    auxilary parameters like LSU cache size should be found in additional
    annotation blocks, if enabled by a corresponding flag;
    2) in SPIR-V, the memory access decorations are applied to the results
    of particular load instructions/pointer access instructions, as opposed
    to memory attributes, which are applied to static entities (static
    variables/struct members).
    
    The "Intel FPGA memory accesses" SPIR-V extension specification can be found at
    https://github.com/KhronosGroup/SPIRV-Registry/blob/master/extensions/INTEL/SPV_INTEL_fpga_memory_accesses.html
    
    Signed-off-by: Artem Gindinson <artem.gindinson@intel.com>

